---
import PathwayLandingLayout from '../../layouts/PathwayLandingLayout.astro';
import { getPathwayHero } from '@/lib/pathwayHeroes';
import { SITE_CONFIG } from '@/lib/seo/config';
import { TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD } from '@/lib/eb5-investment';

const title = 'EB-5 Visa for South Korean Investors | StudentEB5';
const metaDescription =
  'EB-5 for South Korean investors covers investment basics, student timing, and family funding considerations for Korean nationals evaluating a U.S. green card through EB-5.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_CONFIG.url}/pathways/eb5-for-korean-investors`;
const h1 = 'EB-5 Visa for South Korean Investors';
const subhead =
  'A single overview for Korean nationals (investors, students, and families) evaluating EB-5 as a path to permanent residence.';

const teaFormatted = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-US', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'USD',
  maximumFractionDigits: 0,
}).format(TEA_INVESTMENT_THRESHOLD);

const relatedResearch = [
  {
    title: 'EB-5 Immigrant Investor Program: Complete Guide',
    url: '/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide',
    description: 'Filing steps, rural set-asides, and current program structure overview.',
  },
];
const hero = getPathwayHero('eb5-for-korean-investors');

---

<PathwayLandingLayout
  title={title}
  metaDescription={metaDescription}
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  h1={h1}
  subhead={subhead}
  heroImage={hero.image}
  heroImageWebp={hero.webp}
  heroImageAlt="EB-5 pathway for Korean investors"
  relatedResearch={relatedResearch}
  secondaryCtaText="Talk to Someone About EB-5 for Korean Nationals"
  breadcrumbTitle="EB-5 for South Korean Investors"
  keywords={[
    'EB-5 for South Korean investors',
    'EB-5 Korea',
    'Korean nationals EB-5',
    'EB-5 visa South Korea',
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="extraCompliance">
    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed border border-border rounded-lg bg-secondary/40 p-4 m-0">
      EB-5 investment involves financial risk, including potential loss of principal, and does not guarantee a green
      card, visa availability, or any specific processing time. Consult a qualified immigration attorney and a
      FINRA-registered financial adviser for guidance specific to your situation.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <p>
    The EB-5 Immigrant Investor Program offers eligible foreign nationals a path to a U.S. green card through a
    qualifying investment. South Korean nationals, including investors abroad, students on F-1 status, and families
    considering parental funding, typically evaluate the same core program rules: investment amount, source-of-funds
    documentation, project selection, and I-526E filing. Visa numbers are allocated by country of birth; check the
    current
    <a
      href="https://travel.state.gov/content/travel/en/legal/visa-law0/visa-bulletin.html"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">Visa Bulletin</a
    > rather than relying on outdated estimates.
  </p>

  <h2>For investors</h2>
  <p>
    Most investors use the reduced Targeted Employment Area (TEA) investment threshold of {teaFormatted} rather than the
    standard amount. That figure is capital investment only (not filing fees, legal fees, or regional center
    administrative costs). After selecting a project and assembling source-of-funds documentation, investors file Form
    I-526E, with concurrent adjustment of status available in many cases for those already in the U.S. on valid
    nonimmigrant status. Full process detail is on the
    <a href="/eb5-investment-process">EB-5 investment process</a> page and in the
    <a href="/research/complete-2027-eb5-guide">complete EB-5 guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>For students</h2>
  <p>
    Korean F-1 students sometimes evaluate EB-5 as an alternative or parallel track to OPT/STEM OPT and the H-1B
    lottery. EB-5 does not automatically convert F-1 status into a green card; it is a separate petition with its own
    timeline. Starting documentation earlier in a degree program can leave more room to prepare source of funds before
    work authorization windows close. See
    <a href="/pathways/f1-to-eb5-self-sponsored-green-card">F-1 to EB-5 self-sponsored green card</a> for the shared
    student pathway overview.
  </p>

  <h2>For families funding an investment</h2>
  <p>
    When a parent gifts capital to a student or adult child who is the investor of record, USCIS still requires a clear
    lawful trail from the original source through the gift and into the investment. Cross-border remittance and tax
    rules on the Korea and U.S. sides should be reviewed with qualified advisers before funds move. This page does not
    provide tax or remittance advice. Gift documentation, path-of-funds tracing, and early record gathering follow the
    same principles outlined in our funding research:
    <a href="/research/ways-to-fund-eb5-investment-2026">ways to fund an EB-5 investment</a>.
  </p>
</PathwayLandingLayout>
